{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788466513747,"sessionID":"ses_f971d285effeq84jbpOgeGh3gS","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"call_8babf25d8af34af4a8648163","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_go.rs","newString":"const MAX_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlMax[T cmp.Ordered](v []T) tlOpt[T] {\n\tif len(v) == 0 {\n\t\treturn tlOpt[T]{}\n\t}\n\tm := v[0]\n\tfor _, x := range v[1:] {\n\t\tif x > m {\n\t\t\tm = x\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn tlOpt[T]{true, m}\n}\n\"#;\n\n// `slices.SortStableFunc` is what keeps tied keys in their original order, the way jq's\n// `sort_by` reads; a decorated index sort would be the same thing spelled by hand.\nconst SORT_BY_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlSortBy[T any, K cmp.Ordered](v []T, key func(T) K) []T {\n\tout := append([]T{}, v...)\n\tslices.SortStableFunc(out, func(a, b T) int {\n\t\treturn cmp.Compare(key(a), key(b))\n\t})\n\treturn out\n}\n\"#;\n\n// First of tied maxima, the way `tlMax` reads: a strict `>` keeps the earlier entry.on\nconst MAX_BY_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlMaxBy[T any, K cmp.Ordered](v []T, key func(T) K) tlOpt[T] {\n\tif len(v) == 0 {\n\t\treturn tlOpt[T]{}\n\t}\n\tm := v[0]\n\tmk := key(m)\n\tfor _, x := range v[1:] {\n\t\tk := key(x)\n\t\tif k > mk {\n\t\t\tm = x\n\t\t\tmk = k\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn tlOpt[T]{true, m}\n}\n\"#;","oldString":"const MAX_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlMax[T cmp.Ordered](v []T) tlOpt[T] {\n\tif len(v) == 0 {\n\t\treturn tlOpt[T]{}\n\t}\n\tm := v[0]\n\tfor _, x := range v[1:] {\n\t\tif x > m {\n\t\t\tm = x\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn tlOpt[T]{true, m}\n}\n\"#;"},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_go.rs\n===================================================================\n--- /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_go.rs\n+++ /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_go.rs\n@@ -178,8 +178,37 @@\n \treturn tlOpt[T]{true, m}\n }\n \"#;\n \n+// `slices.SortStableFunc` is what keeps tied keys in their original order, the way jq's\n+// `sort_by` reads; a decorated index sort would be the same thing spelled by hand.\n+const SORT_BY_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlSortBy[T any, K cmp.Ordered](v []T, key func(T) K) []T {\n+\tout := append([]T{}, v...)\n+\tslices.SortStableFunc(out, func(a, b T) int {\n+\t\treturn cmp.Compare(key(a), key(b))\n+\t})\n+\treturn out\n+}\n+\"#;\n+\n+// First of tied maxima, the way `tlMax` reads: a strict `>` keeps the earlier entry.on\n+const MAX_BY_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlMaxBy[T any, K cmp.Ordered](v []T, key func(T) K) tlOpt[T] {\n+\tif len(v) == 0 {\n+\t\treturn tlOpt[T]{}\n+\t}\n+\tm := v[0]\n+\tmk := key(m)\n+\tfor _, x := range v[1:] {\n+\t\tk := key(x)\n+\t\tif k > mk {\n+\t\t\tm = x\n+\t\t\tmk = k\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\treturn tlOpt[T]{true, m}\n+}\n+\"#;\n+\n /// Go's `int32` wraps on overflow by definition, and its `/` and `%` truncate toward zero, so\n /// only the zero divisor needs a guard. `MIN / -1` is defined to be `MIN` here, which is the\n /// wrapping answer the other backends were made to give.\n const ARITH_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlDiv(a, b int32) int32 {\n","filediff":{"file":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_go.rs","patch":"Index: /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_go.rs\n===================================================================\n--- /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_go.rs\n+++ /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_go.rs\n@@ -178,8 +178,37 @@\n \treturn tlOpt[T]{true, m}\n }\n \"#;\n \n+// `slices.SortStableFunc` is what keeps tied keys in their original order, the way jq's\n+// `sort_by` reads; a decorated index sort would be the same thing spelled by hand.\n+const SORT_BY_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlSortBy[T any, K cmp.Ordered](v []T, key func(T) K) []T {\n+\tout := append([]T{}, v...)\n+\tslices.SortStableFunc(out, func(a, b T) int {\n+\t\treturn cmp.Compare(key(a), key(b))\n+\t})\n+\treturn out\n+}\n+\"#;\n+\n+// First of tied maxima, the way `tlMax` reads: a strict `>` keeps the earlier entry.on\n+const MAX_BY_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlMaxBy[T any, K cmp.Ordered](v []T, key func(T) K) tlOpt[T] {\n+\tif len(v) == 0 {\n+\t\treturn tlOpt[T]{}\n+\t}\n+\tm := v[0]\n+\tmk := key(m)\n+\tfor _, x := range v[1:] {\n+\t\tk := key(x)\n+\t\tif k > mk {\n+\t\t\tm = x\n+\t\t\tmk = k\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\treturn tlOpt[T]{true, m}\n+}\n+\"#;\n+\n /// Go's `int32` wraps on overflow by definition, and its `/` and `%` truncate toward zero, so\n /// only the zero divisor needs a guard. `MIN / -1` is defined to be `MIN` here, which is the\n /// wrapping answer the other backends were made to give.\n const ARITH_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlDiv(a, b int32) int32 {\n","additions":29,"deletions":0},"truncated":false},"title":"src/emit_go.rs","time":{"start":1788466513744,"end":1788466513745}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The `tail_stmts` match has a wildcard `_ =>` at 795 — so SortBy/MaxBy fall through to `return <expr>` — fine (a sort_by in tail position just returns the expr, which the expr arm handles).. Good.\n\nPython done. Now Go.\n\nGo edits:\n1. Add helpers `tlSortBy` and `tlMaxBy` consts after SORT_HELPER/MAX_HELPER.\n2. uses wiring: helpers table + imports table.\n3. walk arm (line 735 Select arm).\n4. expr arms for SortBy/MaxBy.\n\nGo helper defs:\n\n```go\nfunc tlSortBy[T any, K cmp.Ordered](v []T, key func(T) K) []T {\n    out := append([]T{}, v...)\n    slices.SortStableFunc(out, func(a, b T) int {\n        return cmp.Compare(key(a), key(b))\n    })\n    return out\n}\n```\nHmm — `slices.SortStableFunc` signature: `func SortStableFunc[S ~[]E, E any](x S, cmp func(a, b E) int)`. So `slices.SortStableFunc(out, func(a, b T) int { return cmp.Compare(key(a), key(b)) }))`. Good.\n\n\n\nFor max_by:\n\n```go\nfunc tlMaxBy[T any, K cmp.Ordered](v []T, key func(T) K) tlOpt[T] {\n    if len(v) == 0 {\n        return tlOpt[T]{}\n    }\n    m := v[0]\n    mk := key(m)\n    for _, x := range v[1:] {\n        k := key(x)\n        if k > mk {\n            m = x\n            mk = k\n        }\n    }\n    return tlOpt[T]{true, m}\n}\n```\nFirst-of-ties (strict `>`), matches tlMax. Good.\n\n\n\nImports: `cmp` already imported for tlMax; `slices` for tlSort. For tlSortBy, needs both `cmp` and `slices`. Add `(uses(\"tlSortBy(\", &[\"cmp\", \"slices\"]],` and `(uses(\"tlMaxBy(\", &[\"cmp\"]],`. \n\nHelpers table: `(uses(\"tlSortBy(\", SORT_BY_HELPER],` and `(uses(\"tlMaxBy(\", MAX_BY_HELPER],`.\n\nExpr:\n\n```go\nKind::SortBy { source, param, body } => format!(\n    \"tlSortBy({}, func({} {}) {} {{ return {} }}})\",\n    self.expr(source),\n    self.local(*param),\n    self.go_type(tir::runtime_elem(&source.ty.expect(\"sort_by runs over a dimension\"))),\n    self.go_type(&body.ty),\n    self.expr(body)\n),\nKind::MaxBy { source, param, body } => format!(\n    \"tlMaxBy({}, func({} {}) {} {{ return {} }}})\",\n    self.expr(source),\n    self.local(*param),\n    self.go_type(tir::runtime_elem(&source.ty.expect(\"max_by runs over a dimension\"))),\n    self.go_type(&body.ty),\n    self.expr(body)\n),\n```\n\nLet me make these edits.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_068e9474a001iISThkv74t2XTh","sessionID":"ses_f971d285effeq84jbpOgeGh3gS","messageID":"msg_068e92d83001inGyndkcqElD33"}}
