ted mid-line ( `MAX_HELPER),,` — no leading whitespace needed).\n\nSo — the issue is leading whitespace (tabs vs spaces). The Go emit file indents with tabs. My oldStrings used spaces (8 spaces)., which don't match the tabs. The read tool displays tabs as some width, making me think spaces.\n\n\n\nSo fix: when matching multi-line blocks in Go (and Rust? Rust uses spaces — rustfmt uses 4 spaces? Hmm — the Rust file's helper consts showed 4-space indentation (\"    v.iter()...\"), and my Rust multi-line const edit WORKED. So Rust uses spaces. Go uses tabs (rustfmt on Go? No — this is the Rust emitter file, but the raw string helpers contain Go code with tabs. And the Rust code around (line 696 etc.) — indentation in the Rust source — spaces (4/8). But my Go import-table edit at lines 610-612 — that's Rust source code (not the Go helper strings), with spaces. So why did the multi-line fail there?\n\nHmm wait — let me reconsider. My successful Go edits:\n1. The helper consts (multi-line, with tabs inside — I typed tabs? Let me check what I actually sent. In my edit for Go helpers, I wrote the newString with tab characters? I typed `\\t`? Looking at what I sent — I wrote literal tab-looking sequences? Actually in my tool call I typed spaces (8 spaces? Let me look back: my newString for Go helpers const had lines like `\tout := append(...)` — I used a tab character? Hmm, in my message I wrote it as `\\tout`? No — I literally typed the character sequences. Looking at the message, I see \"\tout := append([]T{}, v...)\" with a leading tab glyph (it shows as a tab in the transcript? Actually it shows `\\tout` — hmm.\n\nOK — I can't reliably tell. Let me just avoid leading-whitespace oldStrings. Use single-line anchors without leading whitespace where possible, or anchors that begin at a non-whitespace char. For the Go import table — I need to insert two lines after the tlMax import line (line 611), before tlEq (line 612).. The tlMax line: `        (uses(\"tlMax(\", &[\"cmp\"]