{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788466588730,"sessionID":"ses_f971d285effeq84jbpOgeGh3gS","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"call_7dd3762dd9004fb7937588e0","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_js.rs","newString":"const MAX_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nfunction tl_max(v) {\n  if (v.length === 0) return \\\"none\\\";\n  let m = v[0];\n  for (let i = 1; i < v.length; i++) if (v[i] > m) m = v[i];\n  return { some: m };\n}\n\";\n\n// The index sort is what makes the sort stable (Array.prototype.sort preserves the order of ties\n// for a 0 comparator, and the indices start distinct, so the tie order is the original one).\n// `cmp` is `tl_str_cmp` when the key is a Str (codepoint order,and only that differs from JS's\n// native string order; every other key type orders with `<`/`>` already).\nconst SORT_BY_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nfunction tl_sort_by(src, f, cmp) {\n  const keys = src.map(f);\n  const idx = [...src.keys()].sort((a, b) => cmp(keys[a], keys[b]));\n  return idx.map((i) => src[i]);\n}\n\";\n\n// First of tied maxima, the way `tl_max` reads (a strict `cmp(...) > 0` keeps the earlier one).\nconst MAX_BY_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nfunction tl_max_by(src, f, cmp) {\n  if (src.length === 0) return \\\"none\\\";\n  let m = src[0];\n  let mk = f(m);\n  for (let i = 1; i < src.length; i++) {\n    const k = f(src[i]);\n    if (cmp(k, mk) > 0) { m = src[i]; mk = k; }\n  }\n  return { some: m };\n}\n\";","oldString":"const MAX_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nfunction tl_max(v) {\n  if (v.length === 0) return \\\"none\\\";\n  let m = v[0];\n  for (let i = 1; i < v.length; i++) if (v[i] > m) m = v[i];\n  return { some: m };\n}\n\";"},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_js.rs\n===================================================================\n--- /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_js.rs\n+++ /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_js.rs\n@@ -203,8 +203,34 @@\n   return { some: m };\n }\n \";\n \n+// The index sort is what makes the sort stable (Array.prototype.sort preserves the order of ties\n+// for a 0 comparator, and the indices start distinct, so the tie order is the original one).\n+// `cmp` is `tl_str_cmp` when the key is a Str (codepoint order,and only that differs from JS's\n+// native string order; every other key type orders with `<`/`>` already).\n+const SORT_BY_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n+function tl_sort_by(src, f, cmp) {\n+  const keys = src.map(f);\n+  const idx = [...src.keys()].sort((a, b) => cmp(keys[a], keys[b]));\n+  return idx.map((i) => src[i]);\n+}\n+\";\n+\n+// First of tied maxima, the way `tl_max` reads (a strict `cmp(...) > 0` keeps the earlier one).\n+const MAX_BY_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n+function tl_max_by(src, f, cmp) {\n+  if (src.length === 0) return \\\"none\\\";\n+  let m = src[0];\n+  let mk = f(m);\n+  for (let i = 1; i < src.length; i++) {\n+    const k = f(src[i]);\n+    if (cmp(k, mk) > 0) { m = src[i]; mk = k; }\n+  }\n+  return { some: m };\n+}\n+\";\n+\n pub fn emit(program: &Program) -> String {\n     let enums = &program.enums;\n     let mut out = String::new();\n \n","filediff":{"file":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_js.rs","patch":"Index: /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_js.rs\n===================================================================\n--- /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_js.rs\n+++ /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_js.rs\n@@ -203,8 +203,34 @@\n   return { some: m };\n }\n \";\n \n+// The index sort is what makes the sort stable (Array.prototype.sort preserves the order of ties\n+// for a 0 comparator, and the indices start distinct, so the tie order is the original one).\n+// `cmp` is `tl_str_cmp` when the key is a Str (codepoint order,and only that differs from JS's\n+// native string order; every other key type orders with `<`/`>` already).\n+const SORT_BY_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n+function tl_sort_by(src, f, cmp) {\n+  const keys = src.map(f);\n+  const idx = [...src.keys()].sort((a, b) => cmp(keys[a], keys[b]));\n+  return idx.map((i) => src[i]);\n+}\n+\";\n+\n+// First of tied maxima, the way `tl_max` reads (a strict `cmp(...) > 0` keeps the earlier one).\n+const MAX_BY_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n+function tl_max_by(src, f, cmp) {\n+  if (src.length === 0) return \\\"none\\\";\n+  let m = src[0];\n+  let mk = f(m);\n+  for (let i = 1; i < src.length; i++) {\n+    const k = f(src[i]);\n+    if (cmp(k, mk) > 0) { m = src[i]; mk = k; }\n+  }\n+  return { some: m };\n+}\n+\";\n+\n pub fn emit(program: &Program) -> String {\n     let enums = &program.enums;\n     let mut out = String::new();\n \n","additions":26,"deletions":0},"truncated":false},"title":"src/emit_js.rs","time":{"start":1788466588726,"end":1788466588728}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"JS Char = a Number (codepoint. And Int = Number, Int64 = BigInt. So key types:\n- Int/Int64/Char → Number/BigInt — ordering via `a < b ? -1 : a > b ? 1 : 0` works (Number and BigInt alike).\n- Str → needs `tl_str_cmp`.\n\nSo for JS, the comparator choice depends on whether the key type is Str. I'll design helpers that take a comparator, to keep the helper generic:\n\n```js\nfunction tl_sort_by(src, f, cmp) {\n  const keys = src.map(f);\n  const idx = [...src.keys()].sort((a, b) => cmp(keys[a], keys[b]));\n  return idx.map((i) => src[i]);\n}\nfunction tl_max_by(src, f, cmp) {\n  if (src.length === 0) return \"none\";\n  let m = src[0];\n  let mk = f(m);\n  for (let i = 1; i < src.length; i++) {\n    const k = f(src[i]);\n    if (cmp(k, mk) > 0) { m = src[i]; mk = k; }\n  }\n  return { some: m };\n}\n```\n\nEmit:\n- Str key: `tl_sort_by(SRC, (E) => KEY, tl_str_cmp)` and `tl_max_by(SRC, (E) => KEY, tl_str_cmp)`.\n\n- Other key: comparator inline: `(a,b) => a < b ? -1 : a > b ? 1 : 0`.\n\nNeed `used.sort_by`/`used.max_by` flags (for helper inclusion), and `used.str_cmp` when key is Str (for the `tl_str_cmp` reference + helper inclusion). Actually the helper selection table uses `used.str_cmp` → STR_CMP_HELPER; the comparator reference `tl_str_cmp` appears in the emitted text, but STR_CMP_HELPER gating uses the `used.str_cmp` flag (which the walk sets for compare ops). So in the walk, for SortBy/MaxBy with Str key, I must set `used.str_cmp = true`. And set `used.sort_by`/`used.max_by`.\n\nHelpers struct fields: `sort_by: bool, max_by: bool`. Walk arm:\n\n```rust\nKind::SortBy { source, body, .. } | Kind::MaxBy { source, body, .. } => {\n    if *which... // no\n}\n```\n\nWait — the walk arm over `Kind::SortBy { source, body, .. }`: I need to distinguish SortBy vs MaxBy to set the right flag:\n\n```rust\nKind::SortBy { source, body, .. } => {\n    used.sort_by = true;\n    if body.ty == Type::Str { used.str_cmp = true; }\n    walk(source, used);\n    walk(body, used);\n}\nKind::MaxBy { source, body, .. } => {\n    used.max_by = true;\n    if body.ty == Type::Str { used.str_cmp = true; }\n    walk(source, used);\n    walk(body, used);\n}\n```\n\nHmm — actually simpler: `used.str_cmp |= body.ty == Type::Str`. \n\nEmit wiring: `(used.sort_by, SORT_BY_HELPER), (used.max_by, MAX_BY_HELPER),` after MAX_HELPER entry.\n\n.\n\n And — hmm, `tl_str_cmp` in the emitted code — but the helper gating for STR_CMP_HELPER is via `used.str_cmp`, which the walk sets. Good.\n\n.\n\nActually — wait: does the sort_by emit reference `tl_str_cmp` textually? Yes — `tl_sort_by(src, f, tl_str_cmp)` — the string \"tl_str_cmp\" appears in decls. But the `uses(...)` mechanism isn't used in JS (JS uses the `used` flags from the walk). The `used.str_cmp` flag drives STR_CMP_HELPER inclusion. Good.\n\n.\n\nLet me now write the JS edits.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_068ea6c2f001NX3FcArdULFA0e","sessionID":"ses_f971d285effeq84jbpOgeGh3gS","messageID":"msg_068ea3a07001IvVEUfWtsZeavy"}}
