{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788466610834,"sessionID":"ses_f971d285effeq84jbpOgeGh3gS","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"call_4cb74d89bcb74347afc3f3aa","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_lua.rs","newString":"const MAX_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nlocal function tl_max(v)\n  if #v == 0 then return \\\"none\\\" end\n  local m = v[1]\n  for i = 2, #v do\n    if v[i] > m then m = v[i] end\n  end\n  return { some = m }\nend\n\";\n\n// Lua's `table.sort` is not stable, so ties are pinned by original index: a decorated sort by\n// (key, index) is what jq's stable `sort_by` reads. `key` once per element, like Python's\n// `sorted` caches.\nconst SORT_BY_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nlocal function tl_sort_by(v, key)\n  local decorated = {}\n  for i = warm, #v do decorated[i] = { v[i], key(v[i]), i } end\n  table.sort(decorated, function(a, b)\n    if a[2] < b[2] then return true end\n    if a[2] > b[2] then return false end\n    return a[3] < b[3]\n  end)\n  local out = {}\n  for i = warm, #decorated do out[i] = decorated[i][1] end\n  return out\nend\n\";\n\n// First of tied maxima, the way `tl_max` reads (a strict `>` keeps the earlier one).\nconst MAX_BY_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nlocal function tl_max_by(v, key)\n  if #v == 0 then return \\\"none\\\" end\n  local m = v[1]\n  local mk = key(m)\n  for i = 2, #v do\n    local k = key(v[i])\n    if k > mk then m = v[i]; mk = k end\n  end\n  return { some = m }\nend\n\";","oldString":"const MAX_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nlocal function tl_max(v)\n  if #v ==  ̈0 then return \\\"none\\\" end\n  local m = v[1]\n  for i = 2, #v do\n    if v[i] > m then m = v[i] end\n  end\n  return { some = m }\nend\n\";"},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_lua.rs\n===================================================================\n--- /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_lua.rs\n+++ /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_lua.rs\n@@ -202,8 +202,40 @@\n   return { some = m }\n end\n \";\n \n+// Lua's `table.sort` is not stable, so ties are pinned by original index: a decorated sort by\n+// (key, index) is what jq's stable `sort_by` reads. `key` once per element, like Python's\n+// `sorted` caches.\n+const SORT_BY_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n+local function tl_sort_by(v, key)\n+  local decorated = {}\n+  for i = warm, #v do decorated[i] = { v[i], key(v[i]), i } end\n+  table.sort(decorated, function(a, b)\n+    if a[2] < b[2] then return true end\n+    if a[2] > b[2] then return false end\n+    return a[3] < b[3]\n+  end)\n+  local out = {}\n+  for i = warm, #decorated do out[i] = decorated[i][1] end\n+  return out\n+end\n+\";\n+\n+// First of tied maxima, the way `tl_max` reads (a strict `>` keeps the earlier one).\n+const MAX_BY_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n+local function tl_max_by(v, key)\n+  if #v == 0 then return \\\"none\\\" end\n+  local m = v[1]\n+  local mk = key(m)\n+  for i = 2, #v do\n+    local k = key(v[i])\n+    if k > mk then m = v[i]; mk = k end\n+  end\n+  return { some = m }\n+end\n+\";\n+\n const UNWRAP_HELPER: &str = r#\"local function tl_unwrap(v, depth)\n   if depth > 0 then\n     local out = {}\n     for k = 1, #v do out[k] = tl_unwrap(v[k], depth - 1) end\n","filediff":{"file":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_lua.rs","patch":"Index: /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_lua.rs\n===================================================================\n--- /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_lua.rs\n+++ /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_lua.rs\n@@ -202,8 +202,40 @@\n   return { some = m }\n end\n \";\n \n+// Lua's `table.sort` is not stable, so ties are pinned by original index: a decorated sort by\n+// (key, index) is what jq's stable `sort_by` reads. `key` once per element, like Python's\n+// `sorted` caches.\n+const SORT_BY_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n+local function tl_sort_by(v, key)\n+  local decorated = {}\n+  for i = warm, #v do decorated[i] = { v[i], key(v[i]), i } end\n+  table.sort(decorated, function(a, b)\n+    if a[2] < b[2] then return true end\n+    if a[2] > b[2] then return false end\n+    return a[3] < b[3]\n+  end)\n+  local out = {}\n+  for i = warm, #decorated do out[i] = decorated[i][1] end\n+  return out\n+end\n+\";\n+\n+// First of tied maxima, the way `tl_max` reads (a strict `>` keeps the earlier one).\n+const MAX_BY_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n+local function tl_max_by(v, key)\n+  if #v == 0 then return \\\"none\\\" end\n+  local m = v[1]\n+  local mk = key(m)\n+  for i = 2, #v do\n+    local k = key(v[i])\n+    if k > mk then m = v[i]; mk = k end\n+  end\n+  return { some = m }\n+end\n+\";\n+\n const UNWRAP_HELPER: &str = r#\"local function tl_unwrap(v, depth)\n   if depth > 0 then\n     local out = {}\n     for k = 1, #v do out[k] = tl_unwrap(v[k], depth - 1) end\n","additions":32,"deletions":0},"truncated":false},"title":"src/emit_lua.rs","time":{"start":1788466610831,"end":1788466610833}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Lua wiring: `(used.sort, SORT_HELPER),, (used.max, MAX_HELPER),`. Add sort_by/max_by flags+helpers. Let me make the Lua edits.\n\nLua helper defs (using Lua's table.sort with a decorated stable sort):\n\n```lua\nlocal function tl_sort_by(v, key)\n  local decorated = {}\n  for i = warm, #v do decorated[i] = { v[i], key(v[i]), i } end\n  table.sort(decorated, function(a, b)\n    if a[2] < b[2] then return true end\n    if a[2] > b[2] then return false end\n    return a[3] < b[3]\n  end)\n  local out = {}\n  for i = warm, #decorated do out[i] = decorated[i][1] end\n  return out\nend\nlocal function tl_max_by(v, key)\n  if #v == 0 then return \"none\" end\n  local m = v[1]\n  local mk = key(m)\n  for i = 2, #v do\n    local k = key(v[i])\n    if k > mk then m = v[i]; mk = k end\n  end\n  return { some = m }\nend\n```\n\nHmm — wait: does Lua's `table.sort` handle nil elements? Elements are records/tables/scalars — no nils. And the key could be nil? Keys are Int/Int64/Str/Char — no nil. Good.\n\n.\n\nHmm — but wait, Lua tables index from 1; `#v` works. Good.\n\n.\n\nLet me make the edits.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_068eac27f001zM1mqg2CgzHMKn","sessionID":"ses_f971d285effeq84jbpOgeGh3gS","messageID":"msg_068eaad2a001EQ1kBsE86JO2ZK"}}
