{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788466657353,"sessionID":"ses_f971d285effeq84jbpOgeGh3gS","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"call_92d8f6f265d84992b4aa1535","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_jq.rs","newString":"Kind::Select { source, pred, .. } => {\n            callees(source, out);\n            callees(pred, out);\n        }\n        Kind::SortBy { source, body, .. } | Kind::MaxBy { source, body, .. } => {\n            callees(source, out);\n            callees(body, out);\n        }","oldString":"Kind::Select { source, pred, .. } => {\n            callees(source, out);\n            callees(pred, out);\n        }"},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_jq.rs\n===================================================================\n--- /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_jq.rs\n+++ /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_jq.rs\n@@ -228,8 +228,12 @@\n Kind::Select { source, pred, .. } => {\n     callees(source, out);\n     callees(pred, out);\n }\n+Kind::SortBy { source, body, .. } | Kind::MaxBy { source, body, .. } => {\n+    callees(source, out);\n+    callees(body, out);\n+}\n Kind::Map { source, body, .. } | Kind::OptMap { source, body, .. } => {\n     callees(source, out);\n     callees(body, out);\n }\n","filediff":{"file":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_jq.rs","patch":"Index: /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_jq.rs\n===================================================================\n--- /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_jq.rs\n+++ /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_jq.rs\n@@ -228,8 +228,12 @@\n Kind::Select { source, pred, .. } => {\n     callees(source, out);\n     callees(pred, out);\n }\n+Kind::SortBy { source, body, .. } | Kind::MaxBy { source, body, .. } => {\n+    callees(source, out);\n+    callees(body, out);\n+}\n Kind::Map { source, body, .. } | Kind::OptMap { source, body, .. } => {\n     callees(source, out);\n     callees(body, out);\n }\n","additions":4,"deletions":0},"truncated":false},"title":"src/emit_jq.rs","time":{"start":1788466657349,"end":1788466657352}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Lua done. Now jq.\n\njq edits:\n1. callees walk arm (line 228 Select arm.\n2. found walk (line 421 Select arm.\n3. expr arms.\n\nFor jq:\n- SortBy: `({} | sort_by(. as {} | {}))` — the filter `. as $t_N | BODY`. Hmm — wait, I need to double check jq's `sort_by` filter semantics: `sort_by(f)` applies `f` to each element. In our body, `BODY` references `$t_N` — so the filter must bind `.` to `$t_N` first. So `sort_by(. as $t_N | BODY)`. \n\nHmm — but wait: is `. as $t_N | BODY` correct? In jq, `. as $x | f` — `.` is the input, `as $x` binds it, then `f` runs with `.` still being the same input. Actually `. as $x | f` — `as` is an expression that sets `$x` to `.` and passes `.` through unchanged. So `sort_by(. as $t_N | BODY)` — the filter receives an element as `.`, binds `$t_N`, then BODY runs (reading `$t_N`). BODY is `($t_N | .[\"key\"])`. Hmm — `$t_N | .[\"key\"]` — jq pipe on a variable — `$t_N` is a value; `|` chains filters, and `$t_N` as a filter emits its value. So yes — works.\n\n.\n\n- MaxBy: `({} | if length ==  ̈0 then \"none\" else {some: max_by(. as $t_N | BODY)} end)`.\n\n\n\nLet me write the expr arms:\n\n```rust\nKind::SortBy { source, param, body } => format!(\n    \"({} | sort_by(. as {} | {}))\",\n    expr(enums, source),\n    local(*param),\n    expr(enums, body)\n),\nKind::MaxBy { source, param, body } => format!(\n    \"({} | if length == 0 then \\\"none\\\" else {{some: max_by(. as {} | {})}} end)\",\n    expr(enums, source),\n    local(*param),\n    expr(enums, body)\n),\n```\n\nHmm — wait, jq's `sort_by` and `max_by` filters are applied to the elements of the array; but `sort_by(f)`'s `f` gets the element. `. as $t_N | BODY` — good.\n\nBut — hmm, actually maybe simpler: since BODY references `$t_N`, and the filter has `.` = element, we could bind: `. as $t_N | BODY`. OK.\n\n\n\nAlso — hmm, one subtlety: jq `sort_by` is stable? jq docs: \"sort_by(f)\" — jq's `sort` is documented as stable (\"sorts... stably\"?). Actually jq manual: \"`sort`, `sort_by(path_expression)` — \"These functions sort their input array... The sort is stable, in that elements with equal keys preserve their relative order.\" I recall that. Good — stable, matches doc.\n\n.\n\nAnd `max_by(f)` injq — jq's builtin: \"`max_by(path_expression)` — returns the element with the greatest value of f\"... tie behavior? jq's max_by implementation: `def max_by(f): reduce .[] as $x (null; if . == null or ($x|f) > (.|f) then $x else . end);` — hmm, let me actually verify. jq's builtin.jq defines: `def max_by(f): reduce .[] as $x (null; if . == null or ($x | f) > (. | f) then $x else . end);`. So for ties (`$x|f == .|f`), then neither branch (`>` is strict) — keeps `.` — the earlier element stays. So first-of-ties. Matches doc. Good.\n\n\n\nLet me make the jq edits.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_068eb783300176hIG2A86Y0uTv","sessionID":"ses_f971d285effeq84jbpOgeGh3gS","messageID":"msg_068eb5dc9001Z7GDzY37hSNNJr"}}
