{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788466681350,"sessionID":"ses_f971d285effeq84jbpOgeGh3gS","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_4bbc1764117e4ae7aebacde3","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"rg -n \"tl_opt|opt_some|opt_is_some|opt_get|typedef.*tl_opt|rec_from_vec\" runtime/toylang.c | head -30"},"output":"1089:int64_t *tl_rec_from_vec(const tl_vec *v, int64_t i) {\n1103:int64_t *tl_opt_some(int64_t value) {\n1109:int64_t tl_opt_is_some(const int64_t *o) {\n1113:int64_t tl_opt_get(const int64_t *o) {\n1117:/* Every element but the first. NULL on an empty Vec -- the same absence encoding tl_opt_some\n1129:    return tl_opt_some((int64_t)out);\n1238: * tl_opt_some uses everywhere else, so a caller unwraps it the way it unwraps an Index result. */\n1249:    return tl_opt_some(m);\n1273:    return tl_opt_some(is_record ? (int64_t)tl_rec_from_vec(v, i) : v->cols[0][i]);\n","metadata":{"output":"1089:int64_t *tl_rec_from_vec(const tl_vec *v, int64_t i) {\n1103:int64_t *tl_opt_some(int64_t value) {\n1109:int64_t tl_opt_is_some(const int64_t *o) {\n1113:int64_t tl_opt_get(const int64_t *o) {\n1117:/* Every element but the first. NULL on an empty Vec -- the same absence encoding tl_opt_some\n1129:    return tl_opt_some((int64_t)out);\n1238: * tl_opt_some uses everywhere else, so a caller unwraps it the way it unwraps an Index result. */\n1249:    return tl_opt_some(m);\n1273:    return tl_opt_some(is_record ? (int64_t)tl_rec_from_vec(v, i) : v->cols[0][i]);\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"rg -n \"tl_opt|opt_some|opt_is_some|opt_get|typedef.*tl_opt|rec_from_vec\" runtime/toylang.c | head -30","time":{"start":1788466681344,"end":1788466681348}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"jq done (3 arms. Now LLVM — the big one.\n\nLLVM needs:\n1. Add `vec_sort_by_key_int`, `vec_sort_by_key_str`, `vec_max_by_key_int`, `vec_max_by_key_str` runtime functions to the C runtime (runtime/toylang.c), and fields in the Runtime struct + module.add_function registrations.\n\nWait — hmm, do I need Str variants of max_by? Max_by keys can be Str (orderable includes Str). The checker allows it. So yes — max_by over Str keys needs a Str-comparing max_by in LLVM. And sort_by over Str keys needs Str comparator.\n\n.\n\n2. Emitter method `sort_by`/`max_by` that computes the keys Vec then calls the runtime.\n\n. This is complex. Let me design the emitter code.\n\n\n\nActually — hold on. Let me reconsider the LLVM approach. The struct-of-arrays layout complicates \"sorting by a projection\". But there's a much simpler approach: implement sort_by/max_by **entirely in the C runtime** by passing a *key-extraction function pointer*? No — the key expr is toylang code, not C. Can't.\n\n\n\nAlternative simpler approach for LLVM: since the checker restricts the projection body, and the LLVM emitter already has machinery to evaluate the body per element (via cursor/value binding — exactly what `map` does)**, I compute a keys Vec: a 1-column `tl_vec` whose column holds the key slots (int64 for Int/Int64/Char, pointer-for-Str... wait — `to_slot` for Str gives ptr-to-int of the pointer; so a key slot for Str is an int64 holding a `tl_str *` — exactly what a `Vec<Str>` column holds. Good — so the keys Vec is indistinguishable from a `Vec<Str>` or `Vec<Int>`: one column of int64 slots.\n\n\n\nThen the C function sorts a permutation by the key column and gathers the source's columns. Actually — even simpler: for the common case where the source element is a scalar (Vec<Int>, Vec<Str>, Vec<Char> — one column), the source itself has 1 column, and sorting the source IS sorting the key column... no wait — for scalars, the key is the scalar itself (`.`, e.g., `sort_by(.)`? Hmm — but a projection body could be e.g. `.age` on a record — the source is a record (ncols = fields count), and keys is 1 column. For a scalar source, the projection is typically `.` — hmm, is `.` a valid body? `.` is `Subject` — but in mapper context, `.` binds to the element; `.` as an expr — how does the checker synth `.`? Hmm — `.` in mapper ctx: `ctx.subject = Some((elem, param))`; synth on `Subject` gives `Kind::Local(param)`? Let me not worry — any orderable-typed body works, including `.` (identity), `.field`, `length(.xs)`, etc.\n\n\n\nOK — generic algorithm in C:\n\n```c\n/* Sort `v`'s rows by the key column in `keys` (a one-column Vec of raw slots), gathering every\n * column of `v` by the sorted index permutation. Stable: ties keep their original row order. */\nstatic int64_t *tl_perm_new(int64_t len);\n...\n```\n\nLet me write:\n\n```c\nstatic int *cmp... \n```\n\nActually let me design:\n\n```c\ntl_vec *tl_vec_sort_by_key_int(const tl_vec *v, const tl_vec *keys) {\n    int64_t len = v->len;\n    /* An index permutation, sorted by the key column. */\n    int64_t *perm = tl_alloc((size_t)len * sizeof(int64_t));\n    for (int64_t i =  ̈0; i < len; i++) perm[i] = i;\n    qsort_r(perm, (size_t)len, sizeof(int64_t), ctx...);\n```\n\nHmm — qsort_r isn't portable (GNU extension, not on macOS/Windows. The runtime already uses plain `qsort` with static comparators (tl_cmp_int64 etc..). For a key column, I can write static comparators that take the key column via a global? That's ugly (non-reentrant). Or write an insertion sort? For small arrays fine,but... Hmm.\n\n\n\nActually — the existing C runtime sorts a Vec's single column directly (`qsort(out->cols[0], ...)`). For sort_by over the source's columns, I need to sort an index array by the key column. The comparator needs access to the key column. Static comparators can't take context unless via globals.\n\n\n\nAlternative: **sort the keys-and-indices together**: build a temp array of pairs (key, index), sort it with a comparator on the pair's first element (qsort works on an array of structs — the comparator takes two pointers to elements of the temp array). Then gather the source columns by the sorted index. This avoids context-carrying comparators entirely! \n\n```c\ntypedef struct { int64_t key; int64_t idx; } tl_keyed_int;\nstatic int tl_cmp_keyed_int(const void *a, const void *b) {\n    int64_t x = ((const tl_keyed_int *)a)->key;\n    int64_t y = ((const tl_keyed_int *)b)->key;\n    if (x < y) return -1;\n    if (x > y) return 1;\n    /* Ties keep original order: idx ascending. */\n    return ((const tl_keyed_int *)a)->idx - ((const tl_keyed_int *)b)->idx;\n}\n```\n\nHmm — `a->idx - b->idx` could overflow? idx are small (len), no overflow. But the sign of a subtraction... idx < 2^63, fine. Actually use explicit compare to be safe:\n\n```c\nreturn a->idx < b->idx ? -1 : (a->idx > b->idx ? 1 : 0);\n```\n\n\n\nFor Str keys:\n\n```c\ntypedef struct { tl_str *key; int64_t idx; } tl_keyed_str;\nstatic int tl_cmp_keyed_str(const void *a, const void *b) {\n    int c = (int)tl_str_cmp(((const tl_keyed_str *)a)->key, ((const tl_keyed_str *)b)->key);\n    if (c != 0) return c;\n    return ((const tl_keyed_str *)a)->idx < ((const tl_keyed_str *)b)->idx ? -1 : (((const tl_keyed_str *)a)->idx > ((const tl_keyed_str *)b)->idx ? 1 : 0);\n}\n```\n\n\n\nThen:\n\n```c\ntl_vec *tl_vec_sort_by_key_int(const tl_vec *v, const tl_vec *keys) {\n    int64_t len = v->len;\n    tl_keyed_int *keyed = len > 0 ? tl_alloc((size_t)len * sizeof(tl_keyed_int)) : NULL;\n    for (int64_t i = 0; i < len; i++) {\n        keyed[i].key = keys->cols[0][i];\n        keyed[i].idx = i;\n    }\n    qsort(keyed, (size_t)len, sizeof(tl_keyed_int), tl_cmp_keyed_int);\n    tl_vec *out = tl_vec_new(len, v->ncols);\n    for (int64_t c = 0; c < v->ncols; c++) {\n        for (int64_t i = 0; i < len; i++) {\n            out->cols[c][i] = v->cols[c][keyed[i].idx];\n        }\n    }\n    return out;\n}\n```\n\nAnd similarly `_str`.\n\nFor max_by:\n\n```c\nint64_t *tl_vec_max_by_key_int(const tl_vec *v, const tl_vec *keys) {\n    if (v->len == 0) return NULL;\n    int64_t m = 0;\n    int64_t mk = keys->cols[0][0];\n    for (int64_t i =  ̈1; i < v->len; i++) {\n        if (keys->cols[0][i] > mk) {\n            mk = keys->cols[0][i];\n            m = i;\n        }\n    }\n    int64_t slot = v->ncols ==  ̈1 ? v->cols[0][m] : (int64_t)v;  /* ??? */\n```\n\nHmm — max_by returns the *entry* (the record/or scalar), not just a scalar slot. What is the runtime representation of an Opt<T> where T is a record? `tl_opt_some` takes an int64 slot... wait, `tl_vec_max` returns `int64_t *` — a pointer to the Opt box. It does `return tl_opt_some(m)` where `m` is the max slot (a raw int64 value). So Opt's payload slot is the value. For a record element, what would the payload be? For max over Int/Int64 — scalar elements, one column, so the max element's value is the slot. \n\nFor max_by over a Vec of records, the result is `Opt<Record>` — a pointer to a tl_opt box whose payload is the record pointer? Hmm — how are records stored? A record value in LLVM is a `tl_rec *` pointer; stored in a Vec's column as an int64 slot (via to_slot ptr-to-int.. So a Vec of records: each column holds raw int64 slots that are actually pointers to field arrays? Wait — the struct-of-arrays: a `Vec<Record>` — the vec's columns each hold the field's values directly (not record pointers). A record element is spread across columns. So there's no single \"record pointer\" to return as the Opt payload!\n\nHmm — but `tl_at` etc handle Opt-of-record via `rec_from_vec`... Let me look at how `select`/`map` outputs a Vec<Record> and how the Opt/record shape works. Actually — for the Opt result of max_by over records, what does the emitter produce? The type is `Opt<Record>`. In the LLVM type mapping, Opt is `ptr` (a tl_opt box with `.is_some` and `.payload` as int64? Let me check `opt_of`/`opt_lit`/`read_slot` for Opt. `read_slot` for `Type::Enum` (incl Opt) does `build_int_to_ptr(slot, ptr, \"elem\")` — so an Opt value is stored as a pointer in a slot. And `opt_some` C function: `tl_opt_some(int64_t v)` — creates a box holding an int64 payload? Let me look at tl_opt_some and the Opt struct.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_068ebd5eb001M42CUHmNdWLx59","sessionID":"ses_f971d285effeq84jbpOgeGh3gS","messageID":"msg_068eb86f5001WN797hs2D8VtUP"}}
