{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788466707195,"sessionID":"ses_f971d285effeq84jbpOgeGh3gS","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_29be6d21d7644a0aa8bcf57a","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/runtime/toylang.c","limit":18,"offset":1236},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/runtime/toylang.c</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1236: /* `max` over a Vec of Int or Int64: the greatest raw slot, Int's already sign-extended so the\n1237:  * i64 comparison orders it correctly. NULL on an empty Vec, the same absence encoding\n1238:  * tl_opt_some uses everywhere else, so a caller unwraps it the way it unwraps an Index result. */\n1239: int64_t *tl_vec_max(const tl_vec *v) {\n1240:     if (v->len == 0) {\n1241:         return NULL;\n1242:     }\n1243:     int64_t m = v->cols[0][0];\n1244:     for (int64_t i = 1; i < v->len; i++) {\n1245:         if (v->cols[0][i] > m) {\n1246:             m = v->cols[0][i];\n1247:         }\n1248:     }\n1249:     return tl_opt_some(m);\n1250: }\n1251: \n1252: /* Collapse one dimension at `i`, `depth` layers down, counting from the end when negative.\n1253:  *\n\n(Showing lines 1236-1253 of 1405. Use offset=1254 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"/* `max` over a Vec of Int or Int64: the greatest raw slot, Int's already sign-extended so the\n * i64 comparison orders it correctly. NULL on an empty Vec, the same absence encoding\n * tl_opt_some uses everywhere else, so a caller unwraps it the way it unwraps an Index result. */\nint64_t *tl_vec_max(const tl_vec *v) {\n    if (v->len == 0) {\n        return NULL;\n    }\n    int64_t m = v->cols[0][0];\n    for (int64_t i = 1; i < v->len; i++) {\n        if (v->cols[0][i] > m) {\n            m = v->cols[0][i];\n        }\n    }\n    return tl_opt_some(m);\n}\n\n/* Collapse one dimension at `i`, `depth` layers down, counting from the end when negative.\n *","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/runtime/toylang.c","text":"/* `max` over a Vec of Int or Int64: the greatest raw slot, Int's already sign-extended so the\n * i64 comparison orders it correctly. NULL on an empty Vec, the same absence encoding\n * tl_opt_some uses everywhere else, so a caller unwraps it the way it unwraps an Index result. */\nint64_t *tl_vec_max(const tl_vec *v) {\n    if (v->len == 0) {\n        return NULL;\n    }\n    int64_t m = v->cols[0][0];\n    for (int64_t i = 1; i < v->len; i++) {\n        if (v->cols[0][i] > m) {\n            m = v->cols[0][i];\n        }\n    }\n    return tl_opt_some(m);\n}\n\n/* Collapse one dimension at `i`, `depth` layers down, counting from the end when negative.\n *","lineStart":1236,"lineEnd":1253,"totalLines":1405,"truncated":true}},"title":"runtime/toylang.c","time":{"start":1788466707185,"end":1788466707194}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"So Opt is a pointer to a single int64 slot, or NULL. The payload is a raw int64 slot. For a `Vec<Record>` element gathered whole (like `tail` over Vec<Vec<T>>, `tl_at` with `is_record`), the payload is `(int64_t)tl_rec_from_vec(v, i)` — a pointer to a rec (an array of field slots). \n\nSo for max_by over a Vec of records, the result `Opt<Record>` — payload should be `tl_rec_from_vec(v, m)` (gathered record), cast to int64. For a scalar element (Int/Int64/Char/Str/Vec/etc.), payload is the element's slot (`v->cols[0][m]`).\n\nHmm — how do I know in the C function whether the source is a Vec of records? The `tl_at` uses an `is_record` flag passed from the emitter (the emitter knows the type`. So my C max_by should also take an `is_record` flag? Or — hmm — can I infer from `v->ncols >  ̈1`? A record with 1 field has 1 column, indistinguishable from a Vec<scalar>. That's exactly why `tl_at` takes the flag explicitly (\"The column count cannot stand in for that test: a record with one field has one column exactly like a Vec of scalars does\"). So my `tl_vec_max_by_key` needs an `is_record` flag (and the emitter passes it based on the source's element type).\n\nWait — but the element type of max_by's source... the checker allows the projection over any Vec (element any type — record, scalar, Vec, enum, Opt??). Hmm — max_by over Vec<Opt>? The projection reads a field of the Opt? orderable-typed body — could be `!` unwrap of an Opt field, etc. — the element could be any type, including nested Vec or Opt or Enum. For the Opt payload, if the element is a record, gather via `tl_rec_from_vec`; otherwise the payload is `v->cols[0][m]` — but for a nested Vec element, the element slot is the inner Vec pointer (`v->cols[0][m]` is exactly that(.— fine (any non-record composite element lives in one column as a pointer slot). For a record element, gather. So a single `is_record` flag suffices, exactly like `tl_at`.\n\n\n\nSo C signatures:\n\n```c\ntl_vec *tl_vec_sort_by_key_int(const tl_vec *v, const tl_vec *keys);\ntl_vec *tl_vec_sort_by_key_str(const tl_vec *v, const tl_vec *keys);\nint64_t *tl_vec_max_by_key_int(const tl_vec *v, const tl_vec *keys, int is_record);\nint64_t *tl_vec_max_by_key_str(const tl_vec *v, const tl_vec *keys, int is_record);\n```\n\nWait — for sort_by, no is_record needed (sorting gathers all columns, no element gathering. Good.).\n\n\n\nNow — hmm, wait: there's a subtlety for max_by with a **nested-Vec or record element** — the Opt payload... For a non-record composite (Vec, Opt, Enum), the element slot (`v->cols[0][m]` — pointer). For a record, gather. For scalar, slot. So `is_record` handles the record case; everything else is `v->cols[0][m]`. Good — matches `tl_at`'s behavior (`is_record ? tl_rec_from_vec(v,i) : v->cols[0][i]`).\n\n\n\nNow the emitter for LLVM SortBy/MaxBy:\n\n```rust\nKind::SortBy { source, param, body } => self.sort_by(source, *param, body, &t.ty)?,\nKind::MaxBy { source, param, body } => self.max_by(source, *param, body, &t.ty)?,\n```\n\nNew methods:\n\n```rust\n/// The key for each entry, as a one-column Vec whose column holds the orderable scalar the\n/// projection produced (an Int-family slot or a `tl_str *` for Str), in the same struct-of-arrays\n/// spelling `sort`'s own element column has.\nfn sort_by(\n    &mut self,\n    source: &Tir,\n    param: LocalId,\n    body: &Tir,\n    result: &Type,\n) -> Result<BasicValueEnum<'ctx>, String> {\n    let keys = self.key_column(source, param, body)?;\n    let src = self.expr(source)?.into_pointer_value();\n    let rt = if body.ty == Type::Str { self.rt.vec_sort_by_key_str } else { self.rt.vec_sort_by_key_int };\n    self.call_rt(rt, &[src.into(), keys.into()], \"sorted_by\")\n}\n```\n\nHmm — but `body.ty == Type::Str` — the key type is Str. For Int/Int64/Char — the int comparator. So the branch is `body.ty == Type::Str`.\n\n`key_column` — computes the keys Vec:\n\n```rust\nfn key_column(&mut self, source: &Tir, param: LocalId, body: &Tir) -> Result<BasicValueEnum<'ctx>, String> {\n    let elem_ty = tir::runtime_elem(&source.ty).ok_or_else(|| \"...\")?.clone();\n    let src = self.expr(source)?.into_pointer_value();\n    let len = self.call_rt(self.rt.vec_len, &[src.into()], \"len\")?.into_int_value();\n    let keys = self.call_rt(self.rt.vec_new, &[len.into(), i64t.const_int(1, false).into()], \"keys\")?.into_pointer_value();\n    let zero = i64t.const_zero();\n    self.emit_loop(len, move |e, i| {\n        if matches!(elem_ty, Type::Record(_)) {\n            e.locals.insert(param, Slot::Cursor { vec: src, index: i });\n        } else {\n            let slot = e.call_rt(e.rt.vec_get, &[src.into(), zero.into(), i.into()], \"slot\")?.into_int_value();\n            let elem = e.read_slot(slot, &elem_ty)?;\n            e.locals.insert(param, Slot::Value(elem));\n        }\n        let key = e.expr(body)?;\n        let key = e.to_slot(key, &body.ty)?;\n        e.builder.build_call(e.rt.vec_set, &[keys.into(), zero.into(), i.into(), key.into()], \"\").map_err(...)?;\n        Ok(())\n    })?;\n    Ok(keys.into())\n}\n```\n\nAnd `sort_by`:\n\n```rust\nfn sort_by(&mut self, source: &Tir, param: LocalId, body: &Tir) -> Result<BasicValueEnum<'ctx>, String> {\n    let keys = self.key_column(source, param, body)?;\n    let src = self.expr(source)?.into_pointer_value();\n    let rt = if body.ty == Type::Str { self.rt.vec_sort_by_key_str } else { self.rt.vec_sort_by_key_int };\n    self.call_rt(rt, &[src.into(), keys.into()], \"sorted_by\")\n}\n```\n\nAnd `max_by`:\n\n```rust\nfn max_by(&mut self, source: &Tir, param: LocalId, body: &Tir) -> Result<BasicValueEnum<'ctx>, String> {\n    let elem_ty = tir::runtime_elem(&source.ty).expect(\"checked to be a Vec\")].clone();\n    let is_record = self.ctx.i32_type().const_int(matches!(elem_ty, Type::Record(_))) as u64, false);\n    let keys = self.key_column(source, param, body)?;\n    let src = self.expr(source)?.into_pointer_value();\n    let rt = if body.ty == Type::Str { self.rt.vec_max_by_key_str } else { self.rt.vec_max_by_key_int };\n    self.call_rt(rt, &[src.into(), keys.into(), is_record.into()], \"max_by\")\n}\n```\n\n\n\nWait — but there's an ordering problem: `key_column` evaluates `self.expr(source)` internally, and `sort_by` calls it twice (`key_column` then `self.expr(source)` again). That would emit the source expr TWICE. For an expensive source that's wrong (e.g., a division inside must trap, run once — but evaluating twice is a semantic change? For a pure language, double evaluation could matter for division-by-zero (must trap once) and for a stream? The source is a Vec (checked), so re-evaluating a pure expression twice is... the checker's linearity applies to streams only; a Vec expr re-evaluated twice — e.g., `v | sort_by(length(.xs))` where v is a function call with side effects? toylang is pure except streams/failures. Double eval of a Vec-typed expr could double-trap a division-by-zero — behavioral difference. So avoid double evaluation: `key_column` should return the src pointer too, or take the src as param. Let me restructure: \n\n```rust\nfn sort_by(...) {\n    let (src, keys) = self.key_column(source, param, body)?;\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nwhere `key_column` returns `(PointerValue, BasicValueEnum)` — src and keys. Hmm — but key_column's `src` is `self.expr(source)?.into_pointer_value()` — evaluating once. Then sort_by uses that src. Good.\n\n\n\nAlso — careful: `result` (the output type) isn't needed for sort_by/max_by in LLVM (the C functions return tl_vec*/int64_t*, and the node's type... the emitter's `expr` returns a BasicValueEnum — for `Opt<Record>` — a pointer (via read_slot? No — the expr value for max_by is the raw `int64_t *` — a pointer. But t.ty is Opt<Record>, and the value is a ptr — matches (Opt = ptr). the map/select outputs — `out.into()` — a ptr (Vec = ptr. Good.\n\n\n\nNow — wait: does `Kind::SortBy`/`MaxBy` expr need the `result` type? For `select`, the expr arm calls `self.select(source, *param, pred)?` — no result type (select returns a Vec, ptr). For `map`, it passes `&t.ty` (result). SortBy's result is Vec<elem> — a ptr. MaxBy's is Opt<elem> — a ptr. So no result type needed. Good.\n\n\n\nNow C runtime functions. Let me write them:\n\n```c\n/* Sort `v`'s rows by the key column in the one-column `keys` (a Vec whose column holds raw\n * int64 slots,the way sort's own element column does), gathering every column of `v` by the\n * sorted permutation. Stable: tied keys keep their original row order,the way jq's sort_by\n * reads. `keys`' slots are raw int64 for Int/Int64/Char and `tl_str *` pointers for Str. */\ntypedef struct { int64_t key; int64_t idx; } tl_keyed_int;\nstatic int tl_cmp_keyed_int(const void *a, const void *b) {\n    const tl_keyed_int *x = (const tl_keyed_int *)a;\n    const tl_keyed_int *y = (const tl_keyed_int *)b;\n    if (x->key < y->key) return -1;\n    if (x->key > y->key) return 1;\n    return x->idx < y->idx ? -1 : (x->idx > y->idx ? 1 : 0);\n}\n```\n\nWait — I need to think about whether tying the idx as a tiebreak breaks the \"ties keep original order\" — yes: stable means ties sorted by idx ascending — correct.\n\n\n\n```c\ntl_vec *tl_vec_sort_by_key_int(const tl_vec *v, const tl_vec *keys) {\n    int64_t len = v->len;\n    tl_keyed_int *keyed = len > 0 ? tl_alloc((size_t)len * sizeof(tl_keyed_int)) : NULL;\n    for (int64_t i = 0; i < len; i++) {\n        keyed[i].key = keys->cols[0][i];\n        keyed[i].idx = i;\n    }\n    qsort(keyed, (size_t)len, sizeof(tl_keyed_int), tl_cmp_keyed_int);\n    tl_vec *out = tl_vec_new(len, v->ncols);\n    for (int64_t c = 0; c < v->ncols; c++) {\n        for (int64_t i = 0; i < len; i++) {\n            out->cols[c][i] = v->cols[c][keyed[i].idx];\n        }\n    }\n    return out;\n}\n```\n\nAnd the Str variant with a `tl_keyed_str` struct. And max_by variants:\n\n```c\nint64_t *tl_vec_max_by_key_int(const tl_vec *v, const tl_vec *keys, int is_record) {\n    if (v->len == 0) {\n        return NULL;\n    }\n    int64_t m = 0;\n    int64_t mk = keys->cols[0][0];\n    for (int64_t i =  ̈1; i < v->len; i++) {\n        if (keys->cols[0][i] > mk) {\n            mk = keys->cols[0][i];\n            m = i;\n        }\n    }\n    return tl_opt_some(is_record ? (int64_t)tl_rec_from_vec(v, m) : v->cols[0][m]);\n}\n```\n\nAnd Str: \n\n```c\nint64_t *tl_vec_max_by_key_str(const tl_vec *v, const tl_vec *keys, int is_record) {\n    if (v->len == 0) return NULL;\n    int64_t m = 0;\n    tl_str *mk = (tl_str *)keys->cols[0][0];\n    for (int64_t i = 1; i < v->len; i++) {\n        if (tl_str_cmp((tl_str *)keys->cols[0][i], mk) > 0) {\n            mk = (tl_str *)keys->cols[0][i];\n            m = i;\n        }\n    }\n    return tl_opt_some(is_record ? (int64_t)tl_rec_from_vec(v, m) : v->cols[0][m]);\n}\n```\n\n\n\nNow — hmm, wait: `qsort` on a zero-length array with NULL pointer — UB? `qsort(NULL, 0, ...)` — technically UB (the pointer must be valid; though glibc handles it.). The existing `tl_vec_sort_int` guards `if (v->len > 0)` before qsort. I'll do the same.\n\n\n\nAlso — the Runtime struct fields + registrations. Add 4 fields:\n\n```rust\nvec_sort_by_key_int: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\nvec_sort_by_key_str: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\nvec_max_by_key_int: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\nvec_max_by_key_str: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n```\n\nAnd registrations:\n\n```rust\nvec_sort_by_key_int: module.add_function(\n    \"tl_vec_sort_by_key_int\",\n    ptr.fn_type(&[ptr.into(), ptr.into()], false),\n    None,\n),\nvec_sort_by_key_str: module.add_function(\n    \"tl_vec_sort_by_key_str\",\n    ptr.fn_type(&[ptr.into(), ptr.into()], false),\n    None,\n),\nvec_max_by_key_int: module.add_function(\n    \"tl_vec_max_by_key_int\",\n    ptr.fn_type(&[ptr.into(), ptr.into(), i32t.into()], false),\n    None,\n),\nvec_max_by_key_str: module.add_function(\n    \"tl_vec_max_by_key_str\",\n    ptr.fn_type(&[ptr.into(), ptr.into(), i32t.into()], false),\n    None,\n),\n```\n\nNote: `tl_opt_some` returns `int64_t *` — ptr. And `i32t` — need it in scope at the registration site — yes (the Runtime::new has `let i32t = ctx.i32_type();`). Good.\n\n\n\nNow — the emitter needs `key_column` method and the sort_by/max_by methods. And the expr arms. And — hmm — does LLVM need a walk pass? The LLVM emitter has no \"walk\" for helpers — helpers are all declared upfront in the module (declared always, even if unused — dead external declarations are fine in LLVM? The C functions are referenced by the emitted module; unreferenced declarations just link fine. So no walk needed. Good — the only LLVM compile errors were the expr match(one site: 1248.** Only ONE Kind match in LLVM (the `expr` match at 1248, plus maybe others with wildcards. The compile errors showed only 1248.** So I add expr arms only.\n\n.\n\n\n\nLet me now write the LLVM edits. First the C runtime.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_068ec3aed001NlU5b3Qn2xYAIL","sessionID":"ses_f971d285effeq84jbpOgeGh3gS","messageID":"msg_068ebdc3b001ILLfs1EQVnaw70"}}
